@H1:5. DETAILED ISSUES
@P:Each entry below states the source of the proposition, the issue, the analysis, the treatment adopted in the draft, and the action required. Cross-references to the Mortgage are to its section numbers.

@H2:A. Collateral, Title, and Priority
@H3:A1. Anson Lane strip — quitclaim conveyance and unquantified title coverage
@P:**Source.** Title Commitment, Schedule A, Section 4 (Parcel Two) and Vesting Chain Note 2; closer's Note 3; Term Sheet § 2.1 and Exhibit A; March 18 markup note § 2.
@P:**Issue.** Parcel Two was conveyed to Borrower's predecessor by quitclaim deed only (Deed Book 1128, Page 443). The Title Insurer states that it is satisfied the City of Charleston held sufficient interest to convey, but expressly disclaims that the chain of title for Parcel Two "is equivalent to that of Parcel One," reserves the right to require an additional premium for extended coverage, and may limit coverage by endorsement. The strip provides the rear access and loading for the building and connects Lots 7 through 9.
@P:**Analysis.** Your three verification points are answered as follows, on the face of the commitment. (1) **Is the strip insured as part of the fee simple estate, without an exception that carves it out?** Schedule B, Part II contains no exception specific to Parcel Two. Exception 7 covers matters shown on the Hampstead Village Plat (Plat Book 41, Page 218), which by its terms does not include the strip, since the strip is described by reference to the Cartwright survey recorded in Plat Book 64, Page 91. On the face of the commitment, therefore, the strip is included in the insured estate. (2) **Is the chain complete?** The commitment shows the quitclaim deed from the City to Hampstead Village Holdings LLC and the vesting chain note states that the "unbroken chain of general warranty deeds ... extending at least sixty (60) years" applies to Parcel One only. There is no examined chain for Parcel Two before 2019; the City's title rests on the ordinance and municipal records. (3) **What endorsement should we seek?** Affirmative coverage for the strip. Because the risk is an identified one (the quitclaim), an ALTA 34 (Identified Risk) endorsement or an express affirmative-insurance provision is the appropriate instrument, not the ALTA 28.1 recommended for the balcony.
@P:**Draft treatment.** The strip is included in the granting clause (Mortgage § 2.1(b)) and in the Land definition (Article 1). Section 6.11 requires Borrower to obtain, to the extent available on commercially reasonable terms, affirmative coverage with respect to the vacated Anson Lane strip, in addition to the standard endorsements. The Note to Exhibit B conditions Lender's approval of the Permitted Exceptions on written confirmation from the Title Insurer that the strip is insured as part of the fee simple estate "without any exception, qualification, or reduction in coverage specific to such strip."
@P:**Action.** (a) Written confirmation from Prestige Title & Settlement Services LLC, copied to you, on the coverage and premium for Parcel Two; (b) if a premium increase is proposed, a decision from Marcus on whether to accept it or to require the strip to be separately insured; (c) confirmation that the quitclaim conveyance carries no reversionary or utility interest adverse to the strip — note that the South Carolina Electric & Gas easement recorded in Deed Book 987, Page 112 is described as affecting the westerly boundary of Lot 9 and the rear of the property, and the strip lies along the rear boundary.
@H3:A2. Balcony encroachment over the King Street right-of-way
@P:**Source.** Title Commitment, Schedule B, Part II, Exception 5 and closer's Note 2; Appraisal § 2.1 and § 5; Renovation Budget line 3.0 ("includes balcony encroachment area repair"; "subject to Board of Architectural Review oversight").
@P:**Issue.** A decorative iron balcony extends approximately 2.5 feet over the King Street right-of-way. The Title Insurer makes no representation as to the existence or validity of any permit, license, or encroachment agreement, and recommends an ALTA 28.1 endorsement if coverage is desired. No permit or license is in the record.
@P:**Analysis.** Three exposures converge here. First, uninsured title risk: absent a 28.1 endorsement, a City-mandated removal or an adverse right in the right-of-way would be borne by Lender. Second, cost and schedule risk: the renovation includes "ironwork repair (including King Street balcony)" and "balcony encroachment area repair," so Borrower will be working on an encroaching structure that may lack authorization, and the City can condition or withhold the BAR approval and building permits on resolution of the encroachment. Third, valuation: the Appraiser treats the encroachment as a minor physical consideration but expressly recommends that the lender confirm the permit status.
@P:**Draft treatment.** Section 6.5 requires Borrower to maintain compliance with any encroachment license or agreement, to notify Lender of any City notice (including any requirement to remove or alter), and to comply with all BAR and permit conditions. Exhibit B conditions approval of Exception 5 on issuance of an ALTA 28.1 endorsement or Lender's express written acceptance of the risk. Section 10.1 requires delivery of all BAR approvals before the affected work begins.
@P:**Action.** Obtain the permit or license status from Borrower before the draft is released; if no authorization exists, obtain a written position from the City (or a letter from Borrower's counsel) and a decision from Marcus on accepting the risk without a 28.1 endorsement. Add BAR approval to the closing conditions (Section 9 below).
@H3:A3. Permitted Exceptions and required endorsements
@P:**Source.** Term Sheet § 5.6 (zoning, access, survey, comprehensive, and future advance endorsements "as Lender may reasonably require"); Title Commitment Requirement 9 and closer's Notes 4 and 5 (survey must include Table A items 1, 2, 3, 4, 6(a), 6(b), 8, 9, 11, 13, 14, 16, and 19 to permit deletion of standard exceptions (b), (c), (d), and (e)).
@P:**Issue.** The record contains no evidence that any endorsement has been ordered or priced, and no written Lender approval of the Permitted Exceptions, although Term Sheet § 5.6 requires Lender's written approval.
@P:**Analysis.** The Mortgage insures a first-priority lien subject only to Lender-approved exceptions. Without (i) a written Permitted Exceptions approval, (ii) deletion of the survey-based standard exceptions, and (iii) the regulatory endorsements, the Title Policy will not support the "first priority, no survey matters" position that the LTV analysis assumes. The closer's Note 4 also imposes a survey scope requirement (Table A items) that must be confirmed with Cartwright, since the Survey as delivered may not include all of them.
@P:**Draft treatment.** Exhibit B lists the seven specific exceptions approved for drafting purposes and contains a note conditioning the approval on the survey, the 28.1 endorsement, and the Anson Lane confirmation. Section 6.11 lists the endorsements required.
@P:**Action.** Order the endorsements now (future advance is the most important), obtain written confirmation that Cartwright's survey includes the required Table A items, and obtain Lender's signed Permitted Exceptions approval for the closing file.
@H3:A4. Survey re-certification timing
@P:**Source.** Term Sheet § 12.1(e); Title Commitment Requirement 9 (survey dated or re-certified not more than ninety (90) days prior to closing); Survey dated January 15, 2025.
@P:**Analysis.** For an April 15, 2025 closing, the ninety-day window opens on January 15, 2025. The Survey is dated exactly on the first permissible day, so it must be re-certified to close, and any closing delay of even one day makes a re-certification mandatory. The Survey must also be certified to the Title Insurer, Lender, and Borrower, and must depict the balcony encroachment and the Anson Lane strip.
@P:**Draft treatment.** The Survey is defined in Article 1 with the re-certification requirement, and Section 6.11 requires delivery of the endorsements that depend on it.
@P:**Action.** Order the re-certification this week; if the closing moves, re-order.
@H3:A5. Priority of future advances; mechanics' lien exposure
@P:**Source.** Term Sheet § 3.3 (Renovation Holdback), § 5.1, § 6.4 and § 8.4 (draw mechanics); Renovation Budget (tenant improvement allowances of $550,000); March 18 markup note § 6.
@P:**Issue.** The Loan is a holdback facility. South Carolina distinguishes obligatory advances, which share the priority of the original lien, from optional advances, which may be subordinated to liens arising between the original recording and the later advance. Mechanic's lien claimants and intervening judgment creditors are the exposure.
@P:**Analysis.** Two protections are required. First, the Mortgage must characterize the Holdback as obligatory and recite the maximum principal amount, so that the recorded instrument fixes the secured indebtedness and the documentary tax. Second, the draw mechanics must produce, at each draw, unconditional lien waivers for prior work, conditional waivers for current work, and a title date-down endorsement confirming no intervening liens. A third, South Carolina-specific protection is available for tenant improvement work: an owner that posts and records a Notice of Nonresponsibility within the statutory period avoids lien liability for a tenant's improvements. Tenant improvement allowances of $550,000 are budgeted, so this exposure is real.
@P:**Draft treatment.** Section 2.8 recites that each Holdback disbursement is an obligatory advance and states the maximum principal amount; the cover recites that the instrument secures obligatory future advances. Section 10.5 imposes the lien waiver, date-down, and inspection conditions. Section 6.15 requires Borrower to post and record Notices of Nonresponsibility within the time permitted by statute for tenant improvement work, and to deliver copies to Lender.
@P:**Action.** Confirm the future advance endorsement is available and priced; confirm with Borrower's construction counsel that the § 29-5-20 procedure will be followed for the Bay 2 and Suite B build-outs; verify the current statutory citation and the form of notice before the draft is released.
@H3:A6. Tenants in possession; absence of purchase rights
@P:**Source.** Title Commitment, Schedule B, Part II, Exception 6; Rent Roll, Lease Abstracts (renewal options and the Bay 2 right of first refusal).
@P:**Analysis.** Exception 6 insures the tenants "as tenants only, without any option to purchase or right of first refusal to purchase." The leases confer renewal options (Lowcountry: one five-year option with a fair-market-value floor; Harmon & Wren: one three-year option at 95% of fair market value; Tidemark: two three-year options) and the Bay 2 right of first refusal, which is a leasing right rather than a purchase right. The estoppel certificates should confirm the absence of purchase rights expressly, so that the title position and the estoppel record agree.
@P:**Draft treatment.** Section 5.5 contains a representation that no Tenant holds a purchase option or purchase right of first refusal; Section 4.5 requires the estoppels not to contain qualifications inconsistent with the Rent Roll without Lender's consent.
@P:**Action.** Include an express "no purchase option or right of first refusal to purchase" representation in the form of estoppel delivered to each Tenant.

@H2:B. Assignment of Rents and Leasing
@H3:B1. Absolute assignment of rents — statute and characterization
@P:**Source.** March 18 markup note § 3 (Uniform Assignment of Rents Act; absolute, not collateral, assignment); Term Sheet § 5.2 (absolute assignment); Title Commitment Requirement 5.
@P:**Analysis.** The draft implements the restructuring you directed. Article 4 effects an absolute, present, and unconditional transfer of the Leases and Rents, states that it is not a collateral assignment made as additional security, and states that it is effective upon execution and recordation. Section 4.2 provides that perfection occurs on recording and that enforcement — including direct collection — arises on default without the necessity of a court order, a receiver, or possession, while preserving the right to seek a receiver. Section 4.3 gives Borrower a revocable licence to collect until default. The word "collateral" is nowhere used to characterize the assignment. One point requires verification: the file cites the South Carolina Uniform Assignment of Rents Act at S.C. Code Ann. § 29-3-100 et seq. The citation and the Act's enforcement and perfection mechanics should be confirmed against the current Code before the draft is released; the operative language has been drafted so that the assignment is effective under general principles even if the statutory reference is adjusted.
@H3:B2. Lowcountry Provisions — no assignment, estoppel, or SNDA provision
@P:**Source.** Rent Roll, Lease Abstracts (Assignment/Subletting: "No provision... does not contain a consent requirement, prohibition, or landlord approval mechanism"; Estoppel Certificate Obligation: none; SNDA: "Lease does not contain SNDA provisions"); Appraisal § 3.2 (notes the omission as "unusual"); March 18 markup note § 3; Term Sheet § 12.1(j) and (k).
@P:**Issue.** The tenant occupying the space subject to the Land Use Restriction, and paying the highest rent per square foot at the Property ($28.50/SF), has no contractual obligation to deliver an estoppel or an SNDA. Both are stated closing conditions.
@P:**Analysis.** You are correct that enforceability of the rent assignment does not depend on the lease's consent to assignment; the risk is practical, and it crystallises exactly when Lender needs it — a default, when the tenant can assert that it never agreed to pay a third party. Your three-step remedy is implemented: (a) a specific representation and warranty that Borrower has the full right and power to assign all Rents under the Lowcountry lease and that no tenant consent is required (§ 4.6); (b) an estoppel delivering within thirty (30) days after closing in which the tenant acknowledges the Mortgage, acknowledges the absolute assignment, and agrees to pay Lender on notice after default (§ 4.6); and (c) a covenant to obtain an assignment-consent amendment at the next renewal, extension, or amendment opportunity (§ 4.6). We recommend going one step further than the draft presently does: make the Lowcountry estoppel an express condition precedent and record in the closing file that it was not waived.
@H3:B3. Estoppels and SNDAs — status by tenant
@P:**Source.** Rent Roll, Lease Abstracts; Term Sheet § 12.1(j) and (k); Title Commitment Requirement 11 and closer's Note 5.
@P:**Analysis.** Harmon & Wren must deliver an estoppel within fifteen (15) business days of request and has covenanted to subordinate to any mortgage "provided that the mortgagee delivers a commercially reasonable non-disturbance agreement." Tidemark must deliver an estoppel within ten (10) business days and "agrees to execute SNDA upon Landlord's request"; note that Tidemark's assignment consent is at the landlord's "sole discretion," so its cooperation should not be assumed. Lowcountry has no obligation at all. Practical consequence: Lender's form of non-disturbance must be defensibly "commercially reasonable" to avoid triggering the Harmon & Wren proviso, and the timing of requests should be sequenced now, not at closing.
@P:**Draft treatment.** Section 4.11 conditions Lender's obligation to deliver a non-disturbance agreement on the absence of an Event of Default, receipt of a satisfactory estoppel, and execution of an SNDA in Lender's customary form. Section 4.5 contains a post-closing cure path if any estoppel or SNDA is not obtained at closing (monthly status reporting), which preserves Lender's position without waiving the condition.
@P:**Action.** Request all three estoppels and SNDAs this week; obtain the form of NDA approved by you before it is sent; do not waive the Lowcountry deliverables without Marcus's written approval.
@H3:B4. Bay 2 right of first refusal — not disclosed in the Term Sheet
@P:**Source.** Rent Roll, Lease Abstracts (Harmon & Wren holds a ROFR on Bay 2, fifteen business days to accept or decline, survives renewal; added by the September 1, 2023 First Amendment); Rent Roll, Vacancy Schedule ("may delay execution by 30–60 days"); Appraisal § 3.2 and § 6.3 (ROFR adds an estimated 60–90 days to Bay 2 absorption and is "factored into" the stabilized projections).
@P:**Issue.** The Term Sheet's tenancy table does not disclose the ROFR and the March 18 notes do not mention it. Lender's leasing covenant (Term Sheet § 10.6) requires consent only for leases exceeding 5,000 square feet or five years, so Lender has no consent right over a 3,800-square-foot Bay 2 lease — the very space the ROFR encumbers.
@P:**Analysis.** The ROFR is a contractual right binding on the landlord that runs with the letting of Bay 2, survives renewal of the Harmon & Wren lease, and (per the Vacancy Schedule) terminates only if that tenant does not renew. It is a matter affecting the Property that a lender would ordinarily expect to see in the rent roll and the estoppel. It also interacts with the Harmon & Wren renewal decision due by December 31, 2025.
@P:**Draft treatment.** Section 4.7 contains a full disclosure representation, a covenant to comply with the ROFR in good faith, delivery of each third-party offer and the tenant's election to Lender, and a prohibition on amending, waiving, or expanding the ROFR without Lender's consent. Section 6.7 requires quarterly leasing reports that address the ROFR process. Section 5.5 and the estoppel requirements capture the right in the estoppel record.
@P:**Action.** Obtain the First Amendment and confirm the ROFR's exact terms; consider lowering the lease consent threshold (see B7); instruct Borrower that no Bay 2 offer may be negotiated without ROFR compliance, so that a non-compliant lease does not create an Event of Default under Section 4.7 or Section 4.8.
@H3:B5. Exclusive grocery use
@P:**Source.** Rent Roll, Lease Abstracts (Lowcountry: "Exclusive use as specialty grocery/gourmet provisions within the Property").
@P:**Analysis.** The exclusivity restricts the tenant mix permissible in Bay 2 and Bay 3, both ground-floor retail bays. The projected Bay 2 rent of $26.00/SF (Vacancy Schedule) assumes a retail tenant that does not compete with a specialty grocer. The scope of the exclusivity is described only in summary form; the lease itself must be reviewed to determine whether the restriction extends to prepared food, gourmet retail, wine, or general grocery.
@P:**Draft treatment.** Section 4.9 prohibits any Lease that violates an existing exclusive use provision without Lender's consent, and Section 8.4 prohibits change of use.
@P:**Action.** Obtain and review the exclusivity clause; confirm the marketing plan for Bay 2 is consistent; disclose the restriction in the estoppel.
@H3:B6. Security deposits
@P:**Source.** Rent Roll, Summary ($69,183 = $18,050 + $15,200 + $35,933, each equal to two months' base rent).
@P:**Analysis.** Security deposits are not "rents" for all purposes and may not be captured by the statutory assignment. They must be dealt with expressly, held in trust, and transferred on enforcement. Separately, the purchase agreement is not in the record, so it is not confirmed whether Borrower receives a credit for the deposits at closing; if it does not, the $69,183 is an additional unfunded use (see 4.5).
@P:**Draft treatment.** Section 4.10 requires the deposits to be held in trust and not commingled, acknowledges the $69,183 amount, and requires transfer to Lender or its designee upon Lender taking possession or succeeding to the landlord's interest.
@P:**Action.** Confirm the deposit credit in the purchase agreement.
@H3:B7. Lease consent thresholds leave the ground-floor bays uncontrolled
@P:**Source.** Term Sheet § 10.6 (consent required for leases "in excess of five thousand (5,000) square feet or for lease terms exceeding five (5) years"); Vacancy Schedule (Bay 2: 3,800 SF, projected five-year term; Bay 3: 3,800 SF, three-year renewal option).
@P:**Analysis.** Each ground-floor bay is 3,800 square feet and market retail terms are five years. Under the Term Sheet threshold, Lender has no consent right over either bay — the two spaces subject to the ROFR and the exclusivity, and the two spaces whose lease-up underwrites the as-stabilized value. The draft implements the Term Sheet threshold (Section 4.8) and does not alter it, because the threshold is a negotiated commercial term.
@P:**Action.** Recommend to Marcus that the threshold be reduced to 3,000 square feet, or that a specific consent right be added for Bay 2 and Bay 3. Instruction requested (Section 8, item 5).

@H2:C. Interest Rate, Prepayment, and Charges
@H3:C1. Yield maintenance — the March 18 formula departs from the Term Sheet
@P:**Source.** Term Sheet § 4.2(b) (greater of 1.00% of the outstanding principal or the present value of the excess of interest at the Note rate over interest at "the treasury rate plus fifty (50) basis points," discounted at the treasury rate); March 18 markup note § 5 (drafting instruction and four open interpretive questions).
@P:**Issue.** The formula you dictated differs from the Term Sheet in three respects. It (i) omits the one percent (1.00%) minimum, substituting a "positive excess, if any" measure with a floor of zero; (ii) measures the premium as the present value of all remaining scheduled payments less the principal prepaid, rather than as the present value of the net interest differential; and (iii) instructs that the fifty basis points be added to the Note rate rather than to the treasury rate.
@P:**Analysis.** Each difference matters. Omitting the 1% floor would give away value and contradicts an express Term Sheet term; the Term Sheet is a binding commitment and § 14.4 permits additional provisions only if not "materially inconsistent" with it. The "present value of remaining payments less principal" measure is a legitimate yield maintenance formulation, but it is not the one the parties agreed, and it can produce a premium that the agreed formula would not. Adding the spread to the Note rate rather than the reinvestment rate inverts the Term Sheet's structure: § 4.2(b)(ii) expressly applies the fifty basis points to the treasury rate. Your interpretive questions are answered by the Term Sheet itself: (c) the premium is the greater of the 1% minimum and the yield maintenance computation, so there is a minimum; and (d) a zero or negative computed differential is cured by the 1% minimum, so the floor question does not arise during the Yield Maintenance Period.
@P:**Draft treatment.** Section 3.9 implements the Term Sheet formula: the greater of (a) 1.00% of the principal prepaid and (b) the present value of the excess of interest at the Note Rate over interest at the Treasury Rate plus 0.50%, discounted at the Treasury Rate, monthly, over the remaining months to the last day of the Yield Maintenance Period. The Treasury Rate is defined by reference to the constant-maturity yield reported in Federal Reserve Statistical Release H.15, interpolated linearly where no published maturity matches, with a fallback to an on-the-run yield reported by a pricing service. Lender's calculation is conclusive absent manifest error. Exhibit D contains a worked illustration demonstrating that no element of the calculation requires the exercise of discretion — a point we will want if the formula is ever attacked as a penalty.
@P:**Action.** Confirm with Marcus: (i) that the Term Sheet formulation governs and your draft language is superseded; and (ii) whether Lender wishes to add a prepayment premium payable on a prepayment after a casualty or condemnation (see C5). If Lender prefers your formulation, the Term Sheet must be amended and the change priced. Verify the enforceability of yield maintenance premiums under current South Carolina authority before release; the formula's determinism is the principal defence.
@H3:C2. Lockout Period — absolute prohibition versus premium-bearing prepayment
@P:**Source.** Term Sheet § 4.1 ("The Loan may not be prepaid ... during the first twenty-four (24) months"; "any prepayment made in violation of this restriction shall be subject to a prepayment premium as described in Section 4.2"); March 18 markup note § 5 ("absolute prohibition ... no exception other than the application of casualty insurance proceeds or condemnation awards").
@P:**Issue.** The Term Sheet is internally ambiguous: it prohibits prepayment absolutely but then specifies a premium as the consequence of a prohibited prepayment, which presupposes that a prepayment can occur.
@P:**Analysis.** An absolute prohibition is enforceable in principle (there is no statutory right to prepay a commercial loan), but it creates two practical problems: a borrower that must refinance because of a due-on-encumbrance or a casualty may be pushed into default with no exit; and an absolute prohibition combined with acceleration can look punitive. The draft resolves the ambiguity in Lender's favour without making the loan impossible to pay: prepayment during the Lockout Period remains prohibited, Lender may refuse any tender, and any prepayment that Lender elects to accept (or that is deemed made under Section 3.11) carries the Yield Maintenance Premium computed under Section 3.9 (which is itself subject to the 1% minimum).
@P:**Action.** Confirm the resolution with Marcus. If Lender prefers a flat premium during the lockout, substitute a stated percentage.
@H3:C3. Casualty and condemnation proceeds applied at par
@P:**Source.** March 18 markup note § 5 ("should be applied at par without any prepayment premium. Confirm this casualty/condemnation carve-out with the client").
@P:**Analysis.** The Term Sheet is silent. Applying involuntary proceeds at par is Borrower-favourable and is a concession that should be a conscious one; the alternative is to apply Lender's elective prepayment premium. Because Lender controls whether proceeds are applied to restoration or to the Debt (Section 12.8), the concession is narrow: it applies only where Lender elects not to permit restoration. This is the position we recommend and the one drafted.
@P:**Draft treatment.** Section 3.11(a), Section 12.8 and Section 13.3 apply casualty and condemnation proceeds at par, without premium.
@P:**Action.** Confirm with Marcus; this is the only point in the prepayment structure on which the draft goes beyond the Term Sheet in Borrower's favour.
@H3:C4. Deemed prepayments and acceleration
@P:Section 3.11(b) treats a voluntary bankruptcy filing and a voluntary prepayment made in violation of Sections 3.7 or 3.8 as deemed prepayments subject to the premium, but does not impose a premium on a prepayment resulting solely from Lender's acceleration following a monetary or covenant default. That is the more defensible line; a premium triggered by acceleration alone invites a penalty argument. Confirm with Marcus.
@H3:C5. SOFR conventions and benchmark replacement
@P:**Source.** Term Sheet § 3.6 (Daily SOFR, successor administrator, prior-day fallback for non-publication; 3.00% floor).
@P:**Analysis.** The definition is silent on compounding (simple average, compounded in arrears, or daily simple with a lookback), rounding, and business-day convention. For an overnight rate those conventions must be fixed or the payment amount is indeterminate — the same determinism problem that threatens the yield maintenance formula. There is also no benchmark replacement mechanism, which for a five-year loan priced off an overnight rate is a material omission.
@P:**Draft treatment.** Section 3.1 defers the conventions to the Note and requires the parties to negotiate conforming amendments to give effect to any benchmark replacement; Section 1.6 provides that the Note controls on the rate, the amount of any payment, and the day-count convention.
@P:**Action.** Confirm that the Note contains the ARRC-style hardwired fallback and a specified compounding and lookback convention; if it does not, the gap must be closed in the Note rather than the Mortgage.
@H3:C6. Usury and the cumulative cost of default
@P:**Analysis.** The Default Rate of 10.85% (5.85% + 5.00%) plus a 5% late charge produces a high all-in cost on default. South Carolina permits the parties to agree in writing to the rate of interest on a loan exceeding $50,000, which should accommodate the structure, but the interaction of default interest and the late charge (each of which is separately described as compensatory) is the kind of combination that attracts penalty arguments. The draft includes a usury savings clause (Section 3.12) and characterizes the late charge as liquidated damages (Section 3.5).
@P:**Action.** Confirm the maximum lawful rate analysis with our banking regulatory group and verify the current terms of the applicable South Carolina statute before release.

@H2:D. Single-Purpose Entity, Authority, and Governance
@H3:D1. Operating Agreement does not support the SPE structure
@P:**Source.** Operating Agreement summary, §§ 1.4 (purpose clause includes "any and all activities necessary, incidental, or related thereto"), 3.1 (no independent director or manager; no bankruptcy consent requirement), 3.2 (Major Decisions do not include bankruptcy filings), 8.1 (no lender consent to dissolution); Term Sheet § 10.1(a)–(g); March 18 markup note § 4.
@P:**Analysis.** The summary confirms each of the four gaps you identified and adds a fifth: the Operating Agreement requires only *reviewed* annual financial statements (§ 6.2), not the audited statements required by Term Sheet § 7.3(a) (see E3). The consequence is that, on the current documents, GDG — through Mr. Graydon — can place Borrower into bankruptcy unilaterally, because a voluntary petition is not a Major Decision and no independent party's consent is required. The single-purpose restriction is also unavailable: the purpose clause permits any related activity, and the Operating Agreement contains no separateness covenants.
@P:**Draft treatment.** Article 7 contains the full SPE covenant set: single-purpose restriction (§ 7.1), eleven separateness covenants (§ 7.2), the Independent Manager requirement with a defined-approval list (§ 7.3), the bankruptcy consent covenant (§ 7.4), a covenant that the SPE provisions bind the Members (§ 7.5), the Operating Agreement Amendment condition and a prohibition on amendment of SPE provisions without Lender's consent (§ 7.6), Key Principal and control covenants (§ 7.7), subordination of member agreements and the drag-along (§ 7.8), and an immediate-Event-of-Default consequence (§ 7.9, Section 15.3(c)). Section 5.9 contains the corresponding representation.
@P:**Action.** Prepare the form of Operating Agreement Amendment (the March 18 note directs that it be prepared separately; it is not in the record and is a deliverable we owe); obtain the full unredacted Operating Agreement, which the redacted summary itself says is required to confirm compliance.
@H3:D2. Member consents — the loan itself requires 75% approval
@P:**Source.** Operating Agreement summary, § 3.2(ii) (Major Decisions include "any financing or refinancing of the Property in an amount in excess of $1,000,000," requiring Members holding at least 75% of Membership Interests); § 2.1 (GDG 72%, Tidewater 18%, Harborline 10%); Title Commitment Requirement 8(b) and (c).
@P:**Issue.** This issue is not addressed in the Term Sheet or in the March 18 notes. GDG holds 72% and the Major Decision threshold is 75%, so GDG alone cannot authorize a $14,750,000 financing of the Property. The unanimous-signature requirement you imposed for the Operating Agreement Amendment is a different and additional requirement.
@P:**Analysis.** The Mortgage's authority representation (§ 5.2) asserts that the Loan has been approved by Members holding not less than 75%. Unless that approval is documented at closing, the representation is false when made, which is itself an Event of Default under Section 15.1(c) and a full recourse carve-out under Section 17.2(a) — an entirely avoidable defect. Title Requirement 8(b) and (c) already requires evidence that the persons executing are duly authorized, but that addresses officer authority, not member consent to the financing.
@P:**Action.** Obtain, before closing: (a) a written consent of Members holding not less than 75% (GDG + Tidewater = 90%, or GDG + Harborline = 82%) specifically approving the Loan, the Mortgage, and the other Loan Documents; and (b) evidence that Tidewater GP Holdings LLC authorized Tidewater to sign.
@H3:D3. Tidewater GP Holdings LLC authority
@P:**Source.** Operating Agreement summary, §§ 2.1 and 4.2(c) and signature page (Tidewater executes through Tidewater GP Holdings LLC as general partner; the GP has "exclusive authority to manage and direct the affairs of Tidewater, including its investments").
@P:**Analysis.** Tidewater's consent to the Operating Agreement Amendment, the joinder to the SPE covenants, and the Section 8.1 transfer restrictions must be executed by its general partner. The limited partnership agreement is not in the record. Delaware law permits a GP to bind an LP, but a lender should obtain a GP authorization certificate or resolution, plus evidence of the GP's good standing and incumbency. Note also that Section 4.2(c) of the Operating Agreement allows the GP to direct transfers of Tidewater's interest to any "successor fund" managed by the GP or its affiliate without the consent of any other Member — a pathway that the Mortgage's change-of-control provision (Section 8.1(c)) is drafted to close.
@H3:D4. Harborline — ownership and management not disclosed
@P:**Source.** Operating Agreement summary, § 2.1 ("The members and managers of Harborline have not been disclosed in this summary").
@P:**Analysis.** A 10% member whose ultimate owners are unknown cannot be diligenced for OFAC, sanctions, or beneficial ownership, and its authority to sign cannot be confirmed. Lender's customer due diligence obligations make this a compliance item as well as a documentation item.
@P:**Action.** Obtain Harborline's Delaware good standing certificate, its operating agreement excerpt identifying its managing member(s), an incumbency certificate, and a beneficial ownership certification.
@H3:D5. Drag-along and indirect transfers
@P:**Source.** Operating Agreement summary, § 4.4 (Members holding 80% or more may require all other Members to participate in a sale; GDG + Tidewater = 90%), § 4.2 (Permitted Transfers to family trusts and to Tidewater "successor funds"), § 4.1 (restrictions apply only to transfers of Membership Interests and "do not purport to restrict ... changes in the internal or upstream ownership, management, or control of any Member entity"); Term Sheet § 10.3 (restrictions limited to transfers of "ownership interests in the Borrower").
@P:**Analysis.** Two gaps. First, the drag-along can commit Borrower to a sale of the Property without Lender's consent, because it operates at the member level; Borrower would then be in breach of Section 8.1 through no act of its own. Second, the Term Sheet's transfer restrictions, as written, capture only direct membership transfers; a sale of Mr. Graydon's interest in GDG, a change of Tidewater's general partner, or a sale of Harborline would not be captured, even though each changes who controls the borrower.
@P:**Draft treatment.** Section 8.1(c) extends the restriction to indirect and upstream transfers and to changes of control, and Section 7.7 identifies the Key Principal and requires retention of control. Section 7.8 subordinates the member agreements and conditions exercise of the drag-along on Lender's consent.
@P:**Action.** These provisions go beyond Term Sheet § 10.3 and should be raised with Borrower's counsel early; they are customary and are within § 14.4's reservation of additional provisions, but they are the provisions most likely to be negotiated. Confirm with Marcus that they are non-negotiable.
@H3:D6. Affiliate fees and cash leakage
@P:**Source.** Operating Agreement summary, § 3.4 (GDG entitled to an asset management fee and a development management fee; terms redacted); summary of distributions (§ 5.1 with an 8% preferred return and a 20% promote).
@P:**Analysis.** With debt service coverage below 1.0x, every dollar paid to an affiliate is a dollar not available for debt service. The Term Sheet is silent on affiliate compensation, which is a significant omission for an asset with insufficient coverage. The draft subordinates affiliate fees to the Debt and prohibits payment while an Event of Default exists or while DSCR is below 1.25x (Section 6.18), and restricts distributions on the same basis (Section 8.7).
@P:**Action.** Obtain the unredacted fee provisions and quantify the annual affiliate compensation; if material, require the fee arrangements to be amended (the draft makes the amendment a condition of closing by requiring delivery of the amended agreements under Section 6.18).
