@H2:E. Financial Covenants and Reporting
@H3:E1. The DSCR covenant is unachievable as drafted
@P:See 4.3 for the arithmetic. The draft implements Term Sheet § 7.1 without alteration (Section 14.1) because the covenant is a negotiated commercial term and the definitions must be fixed; the policy question is for Lender. Four observations inform the recommendation. First, the covenant fails at the first test date on every projection in the record, including Lender's own best case. Second, a covenant that is certain to be breached at inception has limited value as a control and creates a documented default that Lender may not want to enforce — a borrower in default from month 24 has both a negotiating lever and, potentially, a lender liability argument. Third, the covenant interacts with the Reserve and distribution provisions: Sections 6.18 and 8.7 freeze affiliate fees and distributions whenever DSCR is below 1.25x, so an unachievable covenant freezes those payments permanently, which the Borrower will not accept once it understands the arithmetic. Fourth, the Term Sheet's own drafting contemplates that the definitions will be fixed in the Loan Documents (Term Sheet § 7.1), which gives Lender room to calibrate the covenant without a formal amendment.
@H3:E2. Net worth and liquidity — which obligor?
@P:**Source.** Term Sheet § 7.2 ("Borrower shall maintain ... a net worth of not less than $1,000,000 ... and liquid assets ... of not less than $500,000"); March 18 markup note § 6 ("Guarantor minimum net worth of not less than $1,000,000, tested annually. Guarantor minimum liquid assets of not less than $500,000").
@P:**Analysis.** The Term Sheet is unambiguous that the covenants run to Borrower; your note attributes them to Guarantor. Both readings are commercially common — an SPE net worth covenant is in substance a liquidity cushion, while a sponsor net worth covenant is a credit test. The draft follows the Term Sheet (Section 14.2, on Borrower) and cross-refers to Guarantor's separate covenants in the Guaranty, so that neither reading is foreclosed but the Term Sheet is not contradicted. Note that the Guarantor's net worth is stated at approximately $28,000,000, so a $1,000,000 Guarantor covenant would be immaterial in practice; a Borrower covenant is the more meaningful test and is what the Loan Documents should contain.
@P:**Action.** Instruction requested (Section 8, item 6).
@H3:E3. Audited financial statements against a "reviewed" standard in the Operating Agreement
@P:**Source.** Term Sheet § 7.3(a) (annual audited financial statements within 120 days); Operating Agreement summary § 6.2(b) (annual financial statements "reviewed (but not audited) by an independent certified public accountant" within 90 days).
@P:**Analysis.** Borrower is contractually organized to produce reviewed statements on a different timetable from the audited statements the Loan requires. The requirement is not impossible — Borrower can commission an audit — but the cost of an annual audit of a single-asset LLC is real, and the mismatch means the Operating Agreement should either be amended or the reporting covenant adjusted at the outset. This is the kind of term that produces a technical default in year one.
@P:**Action.** Instruction requested: confirm whether Lender will accept reviewed statements with an annual certification and a covenant to provide audited statements upon request or upon a DSCR shortfall (our recommendation), or whether the audit requirement stands.
@H3:E4. Definitions of Net Operating Income and Annual Debt Service require calibration
@P:**Analysis.** The Term Sheet leaves the definitions to the Loan Documents, so the draft supplies them (Section 14.1). Three choices should be confirmed because each changes the stringency of the covenant: (a) a vacancy and collection loss allowance of the greater of actual vacancy or 5%; (b) a management fee cap of 4% of gross revenues and a replacement reserve of $0.25/SF in operating expenses; and (c) Annual Debt Service calculated on a fully amortizing 25-year basis even during the interest-only period. Choice (c) is materially harsher than an interest-only test (0.55x as against 0.72x on the stabilized projection) and should be a deliberate decision rather than a drafting default.
@P:**Action.** Instruction requested (Section 8, item 4).
@H3:E5. DSCR test date — three different dates in the record
@P:**Source.** Term Sheet § 7.1 ("testing shall commence twenty-four (24) months after the date of closing, with the first test date to be the first full calendar quarter ending after the twenty-fourth month following closing (anticipated to be approximately July 15, 2027, for the quarter ending June 30, 2027)"); Rent Roll, Summary ("DSCR Covenant Testing Commencement: April 15, 2027 (Month 24 post-closing)"); March 18 note ("beginning 24 months post-closing (first test date April 15, 2027)").
@P:**Analysis.** The Term Sheet's operative language produces a test on the quarter ending June 30, 2027, certified by the end of July 2027; the Rent Roll and your note produce a test on April 15, 2027. The difference is one quarter. The draft (Section 14.1(c)) implements the Term Sheet's operative language — testing commencing with the fiscal quarter ending June 30, 2027 — because it is the more carefully drafted formulation and because a test date earlier than the Term Sheet contemplates is the kind of change a borrower will litigate. The interaction with the Vacancy Schedule is worth noting: that schedule projects Suite B rent commencement on February 1, 2027, which gives only 2.5 months of rent before an April 2027 test and 5 months before a June 2027 test — either way, an aggressive timeline.
@P:**Action.** Instruction requested (Section 8, item 1).
@H3:E6. Reporting mechanics
@P:Section 14.3 requires audited annual statements, Guarantor's annual personal financial statement, quarterly certified rent rolls (thirty days), annual operating statements, all leases and lease modifications within ten business days, monthly construction reports during the renovation, the annual vapor barrier inspection report, all SCDHEC correspondence, and a compliance certificate. Section 14.6 makes a reporting failure curable within ten days after notice. Section 6.13 requires notice within five business days of eight categories of event, including tenant defaults and any change in the ownership or control of Borrower or any Related Entity. No issue arises on these provisions; they implement Term Sheet § 7.3 and should be confirmed with Lender's portfolio management group.

@H2:F. Renovation, Completion, and Disbursement
@H3:F1. Thin contingency and no equity cushion
@P:**Source.** Renovation Budget, line 5.0 ($200,000 soft costs, $100,000 contingency); Draw Schedule (Renovation Holdback fully drawn at month 14, remaining balance $0; Borrower equity cumulative $905,000); Term Sheet § 3.3 (Borrower responsible for costs in excess of the Holdback) and § 5.5(b) (Completion Guaranty).
@P:**Analysis.** Contingency of $100,000 is 3.17% of a $3,150,000 renovation that includes full HVAC replacement, elevator modernization, and historic façade restoration subject to BAR review on a 1952 masonry building. Five to ten percent is the customary range for work of this scope. The Renovation Holdback is exhausted at month 14 with $0 remaining, and Borrower's $900,000 equity is fully deployed on the schedule (indeed, $905,000 is shown). There is therefore no cushion at either level, and every overrun falls either on Borrower (which has no committed source) or on the Completion Guaranty.
@P:**Compounding issue.** Term Sheet § 5.5(b) describes the Completion Guaranty as guaranteeing completion "within the approved renovation budget and by the Substantial Completion Deadline." Read literally, that phrase could be argued to cap Guarantor's exposure at the budget, which would leave Lender bearing any overrun — the opposite of a completion guaranty's purpose. The Mortgage addresses the point in its definition of Completion Guaranty (Article 1) and in Section 10.10, both of which state that the guaranty is not capped by the Renovation Budget or the Holdback; but the operative language is in the Guaranty, and the Guaranty must say so.
@P:**Action.** (a) Confirm the Guaranty's completion obligation is uncapped and extends to cost overruns regardless of cause; (b) consider requiring an increased contingency or a Borrower-funded cost-overrun reserve as a condition of closing; (c) require evidence of Borrower's ability to fund overruns beyond its committed equity.
@H3:F2. Draw schedule does not reconcile to the budget
@P:See 4.7 for the line-by-line reconciliation. The three defects are: borrower equity of $905,000 against a $900,000 allocation (with the schedule's own total row stating $900,000); a $50,000 shift from the elevator line to the HVAC line, which leaves the elevator scope funded at $425,000 against a $475,000 budget for long-lead equipment required for the certificate of occupancy; and no implementation of the 10% retainage that both the Term Sheet (§ 8.4) and the Mortgage (Section 10.6) require, with only $15,000 of the Holdback remaining at month 14 when $225,000 of retainage would be outstanding under a literal application of the mechanic.
@P:**Action.** Require a corrected draw schedule as a closing condition, tied line-by-line to the budget, with a retainage-compliant draw curve and an identified funding source for the elevator shortfall. The Mortgage does not incorporate the schedule by reference as a condition of disbursement, so the defect does not propagate into the loan documents; but Borrower will submit draws against it.
@H3:F3. Retainage mechanics
@P:**Analysis.** A 10% retainage on a $2,250,000 holdback means that, at any point before substantial completion, 10% of the work in place remains undrawn. Because the Holdback is exhausted exactly at month 14 and substantial completion is due on the same date, the retainage must either be released at the same moment or the schedule must be re-cut. The draft (Section 10.6) caps the retainage at $225,000, requires release against the architect's certificate, final unconditional waivers, a final title date-down, the certificate of occupancy, and completion of punch-list items, and provides that no interest accrues. Recommend a retainage of 10% of hard costs only (not soft costs), which is the more common formulation and reduces the friction.
@H3:F4. Borrower's equity contribution is not ratable in the early draws
@P:**Source.** Draw Schedule, months 1–2 (Lender draws of $25,000 and $165,000 against Borrower equity of $75,000 and $90,000; the schedule's own note: "Lender draws commence in Month 2 while borrower equity is only partially deployed").
@P:**Analysis.** Most construction lenders require the borrower's equity to be contributed before, or at least ratably with, the lender's advances, so that the lender is never the sole source of funds. The schedule has Lender advancing $190,000 by month 2 against Borrower equity of $165,000 — close to ratable but not ratable, and the discrepancy widens and narrows thereafter. The draft requires equity contributions equal to not less than 28.57% of total costs funded to date as a condition of each Advance (Section 10.5(g)) and records the point expressly (Section 10.9). That is a materially tighter requirement than the schedule, and Borrower should be told before closing.
@H3:F5. Substantial Completion Deadline and the completion guaranty
@P:**Analysis.** The Deadline of June 15, 2026 is exactly fourteen months after an April 15, 2025 closing, and the draw schedule's final draw falls on the same day. Any delay in the closing date compresses the construction period unless the Deadline is expressed as a period rather than a fixed date. The draft fixes the date (Article 1) to match Term Sheet § 8.3, and permits extension only for Force Majeure approved by Lender, capped at sixty days, with notice within ten days (Sections 10.3 and 10.17). The following should be confirmed: whether Lender will permit an extension if the closing date slips (recommended: the Deadline shifts day-for-day with any delay in the Closing Date, which is what a borrower will expect and what is consistent with a 14-month construction period); and whether the sixty-day Force Majeure cap is acceptable given hurricane exposure in Charleston.
@H3:F6. Draw conditions and notice period
@P:Term Sheet § 8.4 specifies monthly draws, an AIA G702/G703 certified by the architect, conditional waivers for the current draw and unconditional waivers for prior draws for subcontractors over $25,000, a title date-down, and a Lender inspection. The March 18 note adds a ten-business-day advance notice requirement, which the draft adopts (Section 10.5(b)). The draft also adds four conditions not in the Term Sheet: no default, permits and BAR approvals for the work, LUR compliance for any Bay 1 work, and the equity ratability requirement discussed above. Each is customary, but the LUR condition is specific to this asset and should be highlighted to Borrower's construction team (see H2).
@H3:F7. Interest shortfall guaranty leaves a gap
@P:**Source.** Term Sheet § 5.5(c) (Guarantor guarantees the shortfall in debt service "covering the first fourteen (14) months following closing" to the extent net operating income is insufficient to cover monthly interest payments).
@P:**Analysis.** Quantified, the exposure is immediate and substantial: interest at 5.85% on $14,750,000 is $71,906.25 per month, or $1,006,688 over fourteen months, against in-place NOI of approximately $320,100 per annum ($373,450 over the same period) — an expected shortfall of approximately $633,238 that the guaranty will be called upon to fund. The guaranty period ends in June 2026, but the interest-only period runs to April 2027 and the first DSCR test follows. Months 15 through 24 therefore have no shortfall support: interest of $862,875 per annum against stabilized best-case NOI of $622,772 is a $240,103 annual gap that no one has agreed to fund. That is a credit gap in the structure, not a drafting defect, but it should be surfaced to Marcus because it is discoverable on the face of the Term Sheet and the rent roll.
@P:**Action.** Instruction requested: confirm that the shortfall guaranty period is intentional; consider extending it to the end of the interest-only period or the first DSCR test date.

@H2:G. Sources and Uses
@H3:G1. The quantified funding gap
@P:See 4.5 for the computation. In summary, the Initial Advance plus the non-renovation portion of Borrower's equity equals the purchase price exactly, leaving no source for the $150,000 TI/LC reserve deposit, the $147,500 origination fee, the $54,575 recording tax, or any third-party closing cost — a quantified gap of at least $352,075 before title, legal, survey, appraisal, and environmental costs. The Mortgage assumes the reserve is funded (Section 11.3 requires the $150,000 deposit at closing) and makes a failure to deposit an Event of Default (Section 11.5), so the gap must be closed rather than papered over.
@P:**Action.** Certifying closing statement and evidence of funds before closing; confirm with Marcus how the reserve and costs are to be funded (additional equity, a reduction in the reserve, or an increase in the Initial Advance with the resulting increase in recording tax).
@H3:G2. The Term Sheet's "Total Project Cost" is understated
@P:**Analysis.** Because the Total Project Cost excludes the reserve, fees, and closing costs, the stated 29.6% equity contribution and 70.41% loan-to-cost are each computed on a denominator that omits real costs. On a corrected base the equity contribution is 29.11% and loan-to-cost 69.24% (excluding unquantified items). No one is misled by much, but the unit economics should be restated in the closing statement so that the loan file is internally consistent — particularly since the loan committee is being asked to approve an as-is LTV of 81.04%.

@H2:H. Environmental Matters and the Land Use Restriction
@H3:H1. The LUR requires specific covenants, not "standard provisions"
@P:**Source.** Phase I ESA §§ 5.2–5.4 and § 6(d) (recommends that the lender "require the borrower to maintain ongoing compliance with the LUR as a specific covenant in the loan documents, including provisions addressing the consequences of non-compliance and the lender's remedies"); Title Commitment, Schedule B, Part II, Exception 4 and closer's Note 1 (requires the Mortgage and Environmental Indemnity to "contain specific covenants addressing the SCDHEC land use restriction," including vapor barrier maintenance and periodic inspection and the prohibition on residential use, and requires that "the Mortgage should contain an event of default triggered by any failure to comply with or any modification, termination, or violation of said land use restriction"); Term Sheet § 5.4; March 18 markup note § 6.
@P:**Issue.** The March 18 note characterises the Phase I as having "come back clean except for the controlled recognized environmental condition, which has a no-further-action letter on file," and concludes that "[s]tandard provisions in the mortgage should suffice." Respectfully, that conclusion cannot stand. A Controlled Recognized Environmental Condition is, by definition, residual contamination left in place subject to institutional controls whose continued maintenance is the only thing preserving the regulatory closure. The NFA letter is expressly conditional, and the ESA warns that a breach of the institutional controls "could result in SCDHEC reopening the site." Both the Title Insurer (in a note directed to Lender's counsel) and the environmental consultant (in a specific recommendation to the lender) require bespoke covenants and a defined remedy. Standard provisions would satisfy neither.
@P:**Analysis.** The exposure is asymmetric. The recorded LUR prohibits residential use of the ground floor of Lot 7 and requires the vapor barrier beneath the Bay 1 slab to be maintained — and the renovation work includes HVAC replacement and ductwork throughout the building, façade restoration, and tenant build-outs. Any slab penetration in Bay 1 without prior SCDHEC notice or without protecting the barrier risks a regulatory reopening, at which point the property that was underwritten with no environmental deduction becomes a remediation site. The Title Policy will not help: environmental matters are excluded from title insurance, the LUR will not be removed from Schedule B, and the Title Insurer expressly makes no determination as to compliance. Lender's only protections are the covenants and the full recourse Environmental Indemnity.
@P:**Draft treatment.** Article 9 contains the specific covenant set: no residential use and no use inconsistent with the LUR (§ 9.3(a)); maintenance of the vapor barrier and no penetration or compromise without SCDHEC approval and Lender consent (§ 9.3(b)); annual inspection plus pre- and post-work inspections by a licensed professional, with reports to Lender (§ 9.3(c)); advance written notice to SCDHEC before any Bay 1 slab work, copied to Lender (§ 9.3(d)); written notice to the General Contractor, the Architect, and each subcontractor before their work begins (§ 9.3(e)); delivery of all SCDHEC correspondence within five business days (§ 9.3(f)); no modification, amendment, termination, or release of the LUR without Lender's consent (§ 9.3(g)); maintenance and recording of all institutional controls (§ 9.3(h)); performance of any additional investigation or remediation SCDHEC requires, at Borrower's sole cost (§ 9.3(i)); and preservation of Borrower's bona fide prospective purchaser and contiguous property owner protections and of Lender's own secured creditor protections under CERCLA (§ 9.3(j)). Section 9.4 requires every Lease of any part of the Lot 7 ground floor to prohibit residential use, prohibit slab penetration, require LUR compliance, and permit inspection, and requires delivery of the LUR to those tenants. Section 9.7 makes a breach an Event of Default, and any residential use, any unapproved compromise of the vapor barrier, or any action that reopens the SCDHEC file an immediate Event of Default without cure. The Mortgage also treats the LUR as a Permitted Exception only on the basis of the covenants (Exhibit B, Exception 4). Section 6.11 requires the Title Policy; Section 14.3(g) requires annual inspection reports.
@P:**Action.** (a) Review Article 9 against the closer's Note 1 and confirm it satisfies the Title Insurer, since the Title Insurer's note is addressed to us and its requirements should be met on the face of the instrument; (b) make the baseline vapor barrier inspection a pre-closing or 30-day post-closing deliverable (see H2); (c) confirm the Environmental Indemnity contains the corresponding LUR covenant and that its obligations are full recourse and not subject to the exculpation provisions of Article 17.
@H3:H2. The renovation itself is the LUR risk
@P:**Source.** Phase I ESA § 5.3(b) (any work "affecting the floor slab of Bay 1 must include inspection and, if necessary, repair or replacement of the vapor barrier, with prior written notification to SCDHEC") and § 5.4 note; Renovation Budget, lines 1.0 (HVAC and ductwork for all retail bays and office suites) and 3.0 (façade restoration).
@P:**Analysis.** The budget does not disclose any Bay 1 slab work, but ductwork distribution for a roof-mounted system serving the ground-floor bays, and any new floor finishes or penetrations in Bay 1, can affect the slab. Line 3.0 also includes "balcony encroachment area repair," which is at the front of the building on Lot 7. The risk is therefore live during months 1 through 14, precisely when draws are being made and inspections occur.
@P:**Draft treatment.** The vapor barrier protection obligations run through Article 9 and are made a condition of any Advance for work affecting Bay 1 (Section 10.5(i)); the Lender may suspend work that would violate the LUR (Section 10.12); and the General Contractor and Architect must be notified in writing before their work begins (Section 9.3(e)). Exhibit E's draw request requires disclosure of any work affecting the Bay 1 slab.
@P:**Action.** Add a pre-closing (or 30-day post-closing) baseline vapor barrier inspection to the closing conditions, matching the ESA's recommendation that integrity be confirmed "prior to or promptly following acquisition"; confirm with Borrower's construction team that no slab penetration is planned in Bay 1; require the notice to the General Contractor to be delivered before mobilization.
@H3:H3. Preservation of CERCLA defences; documentation of the Phase II waiver
@P:**Analysis.** As a secured creditor, Lender has the benefit of the secured creditor exemption and of the definitional protections in 42 U.S.C. § 9601(20)(E); Borrower's bona fide prospective purchaser and contiguous property owner defences depend on continuing compliance with all appropriate inquiries, "reasonable steps," and "appropriate care." The Phase I ESA is dated January 28, 2025; components older than 180 days generally must be updated for the inquiries to remain valid, which places a practical limit on a closing after July 27, 2025. The record notes that Lender waived a Phase II ESA by correspondence dated January 22, 2025 — that correspondence should be in the loan file, because it evidences a considered decision not to require further investigation and is the kind of document that is requested in a later dispute.
@P:**Draft treatment.** Section 9.3(j) requires Borrower to take reasonable steps and appropriate care and to cooperate in maintaining Lender's protections; Section 9.6 preserves the secured creditor exemption and confirms that inspection, cure, foreclosure, and deed in lieu do not constitute participation in management.
@P:**Action.** Obtain the January 22, 2025 waiver correspondence for the file.

@H2:I. Insurance and Flood
@H3:I1. $5,000,000 of flood insurance on a Zone X property
@P:**Source.** Term Sheet § 9.3; Phase I ESA § 2 (Zone X, panel 45019C0520J effective December 4, 2020); Appraisal § 2.1 (Zone X, "Community Panel No. 45019C0418J, effective November 4, 2014").
@P:**Analysis.** Flood insurance is not federally required in Zone X, but Lender requires $5,000,000 as a condition of the Loan. That is a lender overlay and is drafted accordingly (Section 12.3), with a covenant to deliver an annual flood zone determination. Two points require confirmation: the cost and availability of $5,000,000 of flood coverage on a commercial building in Zone X (the premium is a Borrower expense not carried in any budget in the record, and should be confirmed as available at a commercially reasonable premium), and the conflict in the flood map data — the ESA and the Appraisal cite different panels and different effective dates. The current effective FIRM panel governs both the mandatory purchase determination and Lender's flood compliance file.
@P:**Action.** Obtain a current flood zone determination from a vendor acceptable to Lender before closing, and reconcile the panel number; confirm the premium is funded and included in the escrow calculation.
@H3:I2. Property, liability, and builder's risk amounts
@P:**Analysis.** Term Sheet § 9.1 requires replacement cost coverage with an agreed amount endorsement but states no dollar amount. The draft (Section 12.1) requires the greater of the outstanding principal balance or full replacement cost. Builder's risk is required at the full renovation budget of $3,150,000 (§ 12.4) and should include soft costs and expediting expense, since a construction-period loss that is limited to hard costs would leave the interest and professional costs unfunded. The CGL limits ($2,000,000/$5,000,000) and the $5,000,000 umbrella are drafted as specified. The thirty-day cancellation notice required by Term Sheet § 9.5 is drafted with the customary ten-day carve-out for non-payment of premium, which is what carriers will actually issue (§ 12.6).
@H3:I3. Failure to insure as an immediate default
@P:Section 12.9 provides that a failure to maintain required insurance is an Event of Default without a cure period (Section 15.3(c)(iv)), consistent with Term Sheet § 11.2(b), which excludes insurance defaults from the non-monetary cure period. That is severe for an inadvertent lapse; consider allowing a five-business-day cure upon proof of reinstatement. Instruction requested (Section 8, item 7).

@H2:J. South Carolina Enforcement and Recording
@H3:J1. Judicial foreclosure; no power of sale
@P:**Analysis.** South Carolina is a judicial foreclosure jurisdiction. The draft disclaims any power of sale in terms (Sections 2.9 and 16.2), provides for a foreclosure action in the Charleston County Court of Common Pleas, preserves suit on the Note, and precludes any provision being construed to confer a non-judicial power. The granting clause uses both title-theory and lien-theory language ("mortgage, grant, bargain, sell, convey, assign") together with a defeasance clause (Section 2.7), so that the instrument is effective regardless of how South Carolina characterises the mortgage estate. The Term Sheet specifies a mortgage rather than a deed of trust; confirm that Lender does not prefer a deed of trust, since South Carolina permits both and the remedies are the same.
@H3:J2. Redemption and upset bids cannot be waived
@P:**Analysis.** Your note directs that the Mortgage waive all rights of redemption. South Carolina's statutory redemption and upset-bid procedures in a judicial foreclosure cannot be waived by contract, and an unqualified waiver in a recorded instrument invites an argument that the lender is overreaching. The draft includes a waiver "to the fullest extent permitted by Applicable Law," with an express acknowledgment that statutory redemption and upset-bid rights are not waivable and that nothing in the Section is an attempt to waive a non-waivable right (Section 16.13). That preserves the persuasive value of the waiver where it does operate while avoiding an unenforceable overreach.
@H3:J3. Deficiency judgments
@P:Section 16.8 preserves Lender's right to a deficiency judgment "to the extent permitted by Applicable Law" and acknowledges that South Carolina law may limit or condition its availability. The Article 17 non-recourse structure limits Borrower's personal liability in any event, so the deficiency right will ordinarily be exercised against Guarantor under the Guaranty or for carve-out liability. Confirm the point with our litigation group if Lender intends to seek deficiencies.
@H3:J4. Execution formalities
@P:**Analysis.** The signature page includes two subscribing witnesses and a South Carolina notarial acknowledgment in the standard form, and a note that the acknowledgment is to be completed at closing. South Carolina conveyancing practice requires witnesses in addition to acknowledgment for instruments recorded in the RMC Office; the Title Insurer should confirm its own execution requirements before the instrument is sent out for signature. Lender's signature does not require acknowledgment, and the draft leaves the Lender block unacknowledged.
@P:**Action.** Confirm execution and acknowledgment requirements with Prestige Title before circulating; verify the current statutory citation for the witness requirement.
@H3:J5. Recording tax fixed by the instrument
@P:**Analysis.** The documentary stamp tax is $54,575 (0.37% of $14,750,000), verified at 4.6. Because the Mortgage recites the maximum principal amount secured and states that Holdback disbursements are obligatory advances, the tax is fixed at recording and no supplemental tax should arise on each draw. Confirm with the closing agent and with the Charleston County RMC Office; if the RMC Office takes the position that the tax must be paid on the obligated amount only, the recital supports the calculation.
@H3:J6. Receivership and mortgagee-in-possession provisions
@P:Section 16.3 provides for a receiver as a matter of right upon default without regard to the adequacy of the security, and Section 16.4 provides for entry and possession. These are standard and enforceable in South Carolina equity practice, but a consent to receivership that is expressed as an absolute right should be reviewed by litigation counsel for consistency with current practice in the Charleston County Court of Common Pleas.

@H2:K. Documentation Set and Consistency
@H3:K1. "Loan Documents" should not include the Construction Contract
@P:**Source.** March 18 markup note § 1 ("Define 'Loan Documents' to cross-reference the GC contract with Beaufort Builders Group LLC ..., the renovation budget, and all reserve agreements").
@P:**Analysis.** That instruction should not be followed literally. If the Construction Contract were a Loan Document, (a) every construction default, however technical and however capable of cure by the general contractor, would become an Event of Default under the Mortgage, because the Mortgage's default provision captures breaches of "any other Loan Document" (Section 15.1(f)); and (b) the distinction between documents that evidence the debt and property that secures it would be lost, which complicates assignment on enforcement. The draft instead defines "Project Documents" and assigns Borrower's interest in them as collateral (Section 2.2), and defines "Loan Documents" consistently with Term Sheet § 14.3. The Renovation Budget is defined separately and incorporated as Exhibit C.
@P:**Action.** Confirm the approach; it is one of the few places where I did not follow a drafting instruction, and the reason is that the instruction appears to have been shorthand for "make sure the construction documents are in the collateral package."
@H3:K2. The assignment of leases and rents is incorporated, not separate
@P:**Analysis.** You elected to incorporate the assignment into the Mortgage to save recording cost and to streamline enforcement (note § 1 and § 3). The Term Sheet permits either approach (§ 5.2) and the Title Commitment expressly contemplates inclusion within the Mortgage (Requirement 5). Consequences to manage: (a) the instrument's title and the granting clause must be broad enough to effect the assignment, which they are; (b) the "Loan Documents" definition must not refer to a separate assignment that does not exist — the draft refers to the assignment as incorporated in the Mortgage (Section 2.4 and Article 4); (c) the UCC-1 should describe accounts and rents; and (d) the closing checklist and the Term Sheet's § 14.3(c) should be annotated to record the election, so that the closing binder is not missing a document the closing checklist calls for.
@H3:K3. Term Sheet internal conflicts
@P:The conflicts identified in 4.9 (guaranty structure, occupancy, DSCR test date, DSCR cure, net worth obligor) should be resolved by Lender in writing before the Loan Documents are finalized, so that the closing file contains a single authoritative statement of each term. We should not resolve them ourselves by drafting, because each is a commercial term.
@H3:K4. Party identification
@P:**Analysis.** Three identification points require confirmation: (a) the registered agent is stated as "Capitol Registered Agents Inc." in the Term Sheet and "Statehouse Registered Agents, Inc." in the Operating Agreement summary, at the same address — one is wrong and the South Carolina Secretary of State record is the source of truth; (b) the Term Sheet's email domain for Mr. Prideaux is "coastalheritagebank.com" while the bank is named "Calverley Heritage Bank" throughout, which should be checked against the bank's charter documents before the Note and Mortgage name the Lender; and (c) the Borrower's identification numbers (SC SOS Filing No. 2024-0011287; EIN 84-6213750) appear consistently and match the Operating Agreement summary, and are recited in the Mortgage's preamble and fixture filing (Section 2.3) so that UCC searches will find the correct debtor.
@H3:K5. Documents to be in the closing binder
@P:In addition to the Loan Documents, the closing file should contain: the Term Sheet; the written Permitted Exceptions approval; the executed Operating Agreement Amendment with all three member signatures, the Tidewater GP authorization, the Harborline authority evidence, and the 75% member consent to the financing; the estoppels and SNDAs; the corrected draw schedule; the re-certified Survey; the final Title Policy or a marked-up commitment with the issuer's unconditional commitment to issue all endorsements; the flood zone determination; the construction contract with all exhibits; the SCDHEC file and NFA letter; the baseline vapor barrier inspection; the insurance certificates naming Lender in each required capacity; the beneficial ownership certifications; and the closing statement demonstrating the sources and uses.

@H2:L. Regulatory and Compliance Items
@H3:L1. Appraisal independence and review
@P:**Analysis.** Lender is a state-chartered bank insured by the FDIC and is subject to the appraisal regulation applicable to its federal supervisor, including the requirement that appraisals for loans at or above the threshold be prepared by a state-certified appraiser and that an independent appraisal review be performed for loans presenting heightened risk. The Appraisal is addressed to Lender and states conformity with USPAP and FIRREA, but the reconciliation defect identified in 4.4 is exactly the kind of issue that an independent review should catch. The Mortgage requires Borrower to cooperate with and pay for appraisal updates (Section 14.5) but cannot cure a defective report.
@P:**Action.** Refer the Appraisal to Lender's appraisal review function before closing and obtain either a corrected report or a documented review conclusion.
@H3:L2. Flood Disaster Protection Act
@P:**Analysis.** If the current flood zone determination places any part of the Improvements in a Special Flood Hazard Area, the mandatory purchase, notice, and escrow requirements of the Flood Disaster Protection Act and its regulations apply, and the current $5,000,000 requirement would need to be tested against the lesser-of statutory calculation. The conflicting panel numbers in the record (I1) mean the determination cannot be assumed.
@H3:L3. OFAC, beneficial ownership, and customer due diligence
@P:**Analysis.** The Mortgage contains representations as to OFAC status and beneficial ownership (Section 5.11). Lender's customer due diligence obligations require certification of beneficial ownership for Borrower and each Member, including GDG (owned by Mr. Graydon), Tidewater (a Delaware limited partnership with a corporate general partner) and Harborline (whose owners are undisclosed). Borrower was formed on January 12, 2024, so its own beneficial ownership reporting obligation to FinCEN arose shortly after formation; confirm its compliance status as part of the file. Because the members are layered entities, the certifications should be completed on the certification form Lender uses for legal entity customers and should be obtained from each Member, not only from Borrower.
@H3:L4. As-is LTV and credit approval
@P:**Analysis.** As-is LTV is 81.04%, above the range at which most banks will lend on commercial real estate without a documented exception. Your closing note asks Marcus to confirm whether the loan committee or board has approved an exception to the bank's underwriting guidelines. That confirmation should be obtained in writing and placed in the file, because the as-is LTV is the ratio most likely to be questioned in an examination. Related: the acquisition-and-renovation structure may raise questions about high volatility commercial real estate classification for capital purposes; the 15% contributed capital and 80% as-completed LTV conditions appear to be satisfied on the record ($6,200,000 of equity is 26.38% of the $23,500,000 as-stabilized value, and the as-stabilized LTV is 62.77%), but the classification analysis should be confirmed by the bank's capital reporting group.
